WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:04.870
Seth Green is barely in his 40s, but he's
been entertaining audiences for more than

00:00:04.870 --> 00:00:05.990
30 years.

00:00:05.990 --> 00:00:11.420
He started as a child actor in the '80s, with
work on TV shows like Tales from the Darkside

00:00:11.420 --> 00:00:16.710
and The Facts of Life, along with movies like
Can't Buy Me Love and Woody Allen's Radio

00:00:16.710 --> 00:00:17.710
Days.

00:00:17.710 --> 00:00:22.399
But he truly became a star in the '90s, appearing
in classics like Buffy the Vampire Slayer,

00:00:22.399 --> 00:00:23.669
the Austin Powers movies,

00:00:23.669 --> 00:00:24.340
"But…"

00:00:24.340 --> 00:00:25.000
"Shh"

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:25.500
"I'm…"

00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:26.000
"Shh"

00:00:26.480 --> 00:00:26.980
"All I'm sayin…"

00:00:26.980 --> 00:00:27.480
"Shh"

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:31.179
and Family Guy, providing the voice of true
idiot Chris Griffin.

00:00:31.179 --> 00:00:36.219
In the late '90s and early 2000s, it seemed
like Green was in every cool thing that came

00:00:36.219 --> 00:00:38.879
out…but he seems to be missing in action
lately.

00:00:38.879 --> 00:00:40.710
Here's what he's been up to in recent years.

00:00:40.710 --> 00:00:43.500
"This is gonna be awesome!"

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:44.530
Robot Chicken

00:00:44.530 --> 00:00:48.320
Stop-motion animation is extremely grueling,
time-intensive work.

00:00:48.320 --> 00:00:52.449
But it's a labor of love for Green, who's
been churning out episodes of Robot Chicken

00:00:52.449 --> 00:00:54.820
on Adult Swim for more than a decade.

00:00:54.820 --> 00:00:59.409
He writes, produces, directs, animates, and
voice-acts on the show, for which he's won

00:00:59.409 --> 00:01:00.600
two Emmy Awards.

00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:06.390
It's basically a sketch comedy show for and
by nerds, making fun of movies, TV, superheroes,

00:01:06.390 --> 00:01:10.920
comics, and cartoons via very short stop-motion
and claymation sketches.

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:16.049
Green has also spearheaded a few specially
themed episodes, targeting things like DC

00:01:16.049 --> 00:01:17.909
Comics villains and Star Wars.

00:01:17.909 --> 00:01:21.000
"You… don't want to get caught in one…"

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:22.750
"Small claims court?"

00:01:22.750 --> 00:01:23.750
"No!"

00:01:23.750 --> 00:01:24.750
"Ahh!

00:01:24.750 --> 00:01:25.750
What was it?"

00:01:25.750 --> 00:01:32.500
In 2016, the show won the award for Outstanding
Short Form Animated Program for its 2015 Christmas

00:01:32.500 --> 00:01:38.479
special, beating juggernauts such as Adventure
Time, SpongeBob SquarePants, and Steven Universe.

00:01:38.479 --> 00:01:42.409
So if you're wondering why you haven't seen
Green on camera, it's because he's been spending

00:01:42.409 --> 00:01:44.869
a lot of time playing with toys.

00:01:44.869 --> 00:01:45.869
Voice over work

00:01:45.869 --> 00:01:50.399
While a lot of actors supplement their careers
with voice work, or fall back on it when offers

00:01:50.399 --> 00:01:55.350
for onscreen roles aren't as plentiful, Green
has always done a ton of voice acting — starting

00:01:55.350 --> 00:02:00.009
when he was a teenager, on the syndicated
cartoon "The Comic Strip" segment - entitled

00:02:00.009 --> 00:02:01.070
Karate Kat.

00:02:01.070 --> 00:02:05.759
He of course has lent his vocal chops to hundreds
of characters on his show Robot Chicken, from

00:02:05.759 --> 00:02:08.600
original characters to celebrity impressions.

00:02:08.600 --> 00:02:12.820
Of course, his voice has also appeared in
more than 200 episodes of Family Guy since

00:02:12.820 --> 00:02:14.180
1999.

00:02:14.180 --> 00:02:18.430
He's got an impressive vocal résumé, in
other words, and he's highly sought after.

00:02:18.430 --> 00:02:22.780
Green provided the voice of Rocket Raccoon
for episodes of both Avengers Assemble and

00:02:22.780 --> 00:02:25.030
Hulk and the Agents of S.M.A.S.H.

00:02:25.030 --> 00:02:28.660
And that memorable Howard the Duck cameo in
the post-credits sequence of Guardians of

00:02:28.660 --> 00:02:29.660
the Galaxy?

00:02:29.660 --> 00:02:31.700
"Why do you let it lick you like that for?

00:02:33.500 --> 00:02:34.000
Gross."

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:35.610
That was Seth Green.

00:02:35.610 --> 00:02:40.370
He can also be heard as Leonardo in Nickelodeon's
Teenage Mutant Ninja Turtles reboot.

00:02:40.370 --> 00:02:43.080
Basically, he’s a king of cartoons.

00:02:43.080 --> 00:02:44.370
Secret Star Wars series

00:02:44.370 --> 00:02:49.650
There's an unaired Star Wars TV series that
already has more than three dozen episodes

00:02:49.650 --> 00:02:50.650
produced.

00:02:50.650 --> 00:02:55.260
Called Star Wars: Detours, it's an animated
comedy set in the Star Wars universe.

00:02:55.260 --> 00:03:00.020
Producers wisely signed up Green, who's both
a notable voice actor and, as the many Star

00:03:00.020 --> 00:03:03.170
Wars sketches on Robot Chicken prove, a huge
fan.

00:03:03.170 --> 00:03:04.560
The problem with Detours?

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:05.560
Disney.

00:03:05.560 --> 00:03:10.760
According to Green, there are "39 finished
episodes and around 62 finished scripts."

00:03:10.760 --> 00:03:15.700
But it was made before Disney bought LucasFilm
and decided to make more Star Wars movies.

00:03:15.700 --> 00:03:21.000
Green, who plays none other than Obi-Wan Kenobi
in the series, says it's "on hold," but that

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:25.860
it might actually likely come out now that
a couple new Star Wars movies have been released.

00:03:25.860 --> 00:03:26.860
Bombed sitcom

00:03:26.860 --> 00:03:32.260
Meanwhile, Green doesn't always hide behind
clay, action figures, and comic book characters.

00:03:32.260 --> 00:03:37.890
He attempted to return to regular live-action
acting work in 2013, starring on Fox's three-camera

00:03:37.890 --> 00:03:39.650
sitcom Dads.

00:03:39.650 --> 00:03:43.880
Created by two writers from one of Green's
other shows, Family Guy, and produced by Family

00:03:43.880 --> 00:03:48.220
Guy creator Seth MacFarlane, Dads was largely
loathed by critics.

00:03:48.220 --> 00:03:52.690
The series, about two friends who run a video
game company and take in their elderly fathers,

00:03:52.690 --> 00:03:58.171
was called a "racist, sexist, unfunny disgrace"
by BuzzFeed, and "the worst new comedy of

00:03:58.171 --> 00:04:00.500
the fall" by E! Online.

00:04:00.500 --> 00:04:06.680
Audiences stayed away, too — it finished
the 2013-14 season at #111 in the ratings.

00:04:06.680 --> 00:04:12.540
It performed so poorly that Fox cut back its
initial order of 22 episodes to 19…before

00:04:12.540 --> 00:04:14.830
canceling it entirely.

00:04:14.830 --> 00:04:16.160
Cartoon creator

00:04:16.160 --> 00:04:20.829
In addition to controlling the Robot Chicken
universe, Green has his hands in multiple

00:04:20.829 --> 00:04:21.829
cartoons.

00:04:21.829 --> 00:04:26.860
Since Green is a big pro wrestling fan — he
even once hosted the Slammy Awards — WWE

00:04:26.860 --> 00:04:32.469
chairman Vince McMahon approached Green when
he had an idea for an animated show: Camp

00:04:32.469 --> 00:04:33.469
WWE.

00:04:33.469 --> 00:04:39.229
It's set at a summer camp, where McMahon is
the director and WWE stars past and present

00:04:39.229 --> 00:04:44.150
are counselors and campers alike — except
they're, like, little kids.

00:04:44.150 --> 00:04:48.349
Green created and produces the show and some
of the Wrestler Babies are portrayed by the

00:04:48.349 --> 00:04:50.759
wrestlers themselves, including Sgt.

00:04:50.759 --> 00:04:52.520
Slaughter and Ric Flair.

00:04:52.520 --> 00:04:56.860
When he’s not producing for WWE, he's an
executive producer on SuperMansion.

00:04:56.860 --> 00:05:01.669
It’s a show about a once-mighty superhero
collective whose members are getting old and

00:05:01.669 --> 00:05:04.309
their powers aren’t quite what they used
to be.

00:05:04.309 --> 00:05:08.990
Appearing on Crackle, it features the amazing
voice talent of Bryan Cranston and Keegan-Michael

00:05:08.990 --> 00:05:10.270
Key.

00:05:10.270 --> 00:05:11.270
Wedding bells

00:05:11.270 --> 00:05:16.789
In 2007, Green was signing autographs for
an event at Golden Apple Comics in Los Angeles

00:05:16.789 --> 00:05:21.430
while Clare Grant, a filmmaker and actress
who's also part of the web video collective

00:05:21.430 --> 00:05:24.270
Team Unicorn, was there to take photographs.

00:05:24.270 --> 00:05:28.139
They met again at Comic-Con and realized how
much they had in common — they liked the

00:05:28.139 --> 00:05:33.490
same geeky TV shows, comic books, and toys,
and put in the same elaborate order at In-N-Out

00:05:33.490 --> 00:05:34.490
Burger.

00:05:34.490 --> 00:05:38.620
Green says they really fell in love during
a two-week Robot Chicken touring roadshow.

00:05:38.620 --> 00:05:43.340
They had their wedding in May 2010 and their
reception was decked out with action figures

00:05:43.340 --> 00:05:45.120
for every guest to take home.

00:05:45.120 --> 00:05:47.600
Ah, what sweet nerd love.

00:05:47.600 --> 00:05:49.539
What's next for Seth Green

00:05:49.539 --> 00:05:54.349
Green seems to be content, and rightfully
so, with cranking out Robot Chicken and Family

00:05:54.349 --> 00:05:56.879
Guy episodes until somebody tells him to stop.

00:05:56.879 --> 00:06:01.560
Plus, he still surprises TV viewers by getting
out in front of the camera on occasion.

00:06:01.560 --> 00:06:06.479
He's also been cast in the highly anticipated
movie, A Futile &amp; Stupid Gesture, the story

00:06:06.479 --> 00:06:12.060
of Doug Kenney, co-founder of the highly influential
National Lampoon magazine in the '70s and

00:06:12.060 --> 00:06:14.310
the generation of comedy stars it created.

00:06:14.310 --> 00:06:17.740
Looks like Green only accepts the coolest
projects of the decade.

00:06:17.740 --> 00:06:19.080
Thanks for watching!

00:06:19.080 --> 00:06:22.009
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:06:22.009 --> 00:06:24.280
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love too!

